WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.540
The word eval deserves special attention because it changes the trust model of a script.

2
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.760
It asks Bash to take generated text and interpret it again as shell code.

3
00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:18.540
That second interpretation step is where data can become syntax, and where reviewers can lose confidence that they understand what will run.

4
00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:22.580
In day-to-day automation, eval is usually not needed.

5
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:29.080
It is often used as a shortcut for ordinary control flow, reusable behavior, or argument construction.

6
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.220
Those problems have safer designs.

7
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.480
Use functions for reusable behavior.

8
00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.780
Use a case statement to select a known action.

9
00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:40.360
Use arrays to build argument lists.

10
00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.500
Use fixed commands with validated operands.

11
00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:47.140
The risk is not limited to hostile input.

12
00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:54.280
Re-parsing can break a script when values contain spaces, pattern characters, quotes, or other text the shell treats specially.

13
00:00:54.380 --> 00:01:01.740
It also makes maintenance harder because future changes may introduce new expansion behavior that was not part of the original mental model.

14
00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:08.020
When a design appears to require dynamic interpretation, pause and separate the requirements.

15
00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.600
Are you selecting one of several known behaviors?

16
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:11.660
Use a mapping.

17
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.920
Are you adding optional arguments?

18
00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:14.980
Use an array.

19
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:16.600
Are you forwarding parameters?

20
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.780
Preserve them with "$@".

21
00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.200
Are you trying to store shell code in data?

22
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:24.740
That is usually a sign the script needs a different design.

23
00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:39.580
There are advanced shell scenarios where dynamic evaluation exists, but they should be rare, isolated, heavily reviewed, and unnecessary for normal administrative scripts, CI jobs, deployment wrappers, or maintenance automation.

24
00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:45.040
For most teams, the better default is simple: do not use eval for command execution.

25
00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.880
The defensive standard is reviewability.

26
00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:54.140
A person reading the script should not have to reconstruct a second hidden shell program in their head.

27
00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:01.600
If the script can express the action with known functions, fixed commands, arrays, and validation, that is the path to choose.
